 progressive training, and sparse MoE architecture.\nL87: \nL88: Generated by cite37†Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct L89: We introduce cite38†SAIL-VL2 , an open-suite cite39†vision-language foundation model (LVM) for comprehensive multimodal understanding and reasoning. As the successor to cite40†SAIL-VL , cite38†SAIL-VL2 achieves state-of-the-art performance at the 2B and 8B cite41†parameter scales across diverse cite42†image and video benchmarks , demonstrating strong capabilities from cite43†fine-grained perception to cite44†complex reasoning . Three core innovations drive its effectiveness.\nL90: First, a cite45†large-scale data curation pipeline with cite46†scoring and filtering strategies enhances both quality and distribution across captioning, OCR, QA, and video data, improving training efficiency. Second, a cite47†progressive training framework begins with a powerful pre-trained vision encoder (cite48†SAIL-ViT ), advances through multimodal pre-training, and culminates in a cite49†thinking-fusion SFT-RL hybrid paradigm that systematically strengthens model capabilities.\nL91: Third, architectural advances extend beyond cite50†dense LLMs to cite51†efficient sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) designs. With these contributions, cite38†SAIL-VL2 demonstrates competitive performance across 106 datasets and achieves state-of-the-art results on challenging reasoning benchmarks such as cite52†MMMU and cite53†MathVista .\nL92: Furthermore, on the OpenCompass leaderboard, cite38†SAIL-VL2 -2B ranks first among officially released open-source models under the 4B parameter scale, while serving as an efficient and extensible foundation for the open-source multimodal community.\nL93: cite54†View arXiv page†arxiv.org cite55†View PDF†arxiv.org cite56†Project page cite28†Add to collection L94: ### Community\nL95: \nL96: cite57†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L97: \nL98: cite26†taesiri L99: \nL100: Paper submitter cite58†Sep 18, 2025 L101: We introduce SAIL-VL2, an open-suite vision-language foundation model (LVM) for comprehensive multimodal understanding and reasoning. As the successor to SAIL-VL, SAIL-VL2 achieves state-of-the-art performance at the 2B and 8B parameter scales across diverse image and video benchmarks, demonstrating strong capabilities from fine-grained perception to complex reasoning. Three core innovations drive its effectiveness.\nL102: First, a large-scale data curation pipeline with scoring and filtering strategies enhances both quality and distribution across captioning, OCR, QA, and video data, improving training efficiency. Second, a progressive training framework begins with a powerful pre-trained vision encoder (SAIL-ViT), advances through multimodal pre-training, and culminates in a thinking-fusion SFT-RL hybrid paradigm that systematically strengthens model capabilities.\nL103: Third, architectural advances extend beyond dense LLMs to efficient sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) designs. With these contributions, SAIL-VL2 demonstrates competitive performance across 106 datasets and achieves state-of-the-art results on challenging reasoning benchmarks such as MMMU and MathVista.\nL104: Furthermore, on the OpenCompass leaderboard, SAIL-VL2-2B ranks first among officially released open-source models under the 4B parameter scale, while serving as an efficient and extensible foundation for the open-source multimodal community.\nL105: Reply\nL106: \nL107: cite59†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L108: \nL109: cite60†librarian-bot L110: \nL111: cite61†Sep 19, 2025 L112: \nL113: This is an automated message from the cite62†Librarian Bot . I found the following papers similar to this paper.\nL114: \nL115: The following papers were recommended by the Semantic Scholar API\nL116:   * cite63†MoCHA: Advanced Vision-Language Reasoning with MoE Connector and Hierarchical Group Attention (2025)\nL117:   * cite64†MoIIE: Mixture of Int